reynolds-number-scaling-ident-b08188f8.limits_and_boundary:
As the Reynolds number (
Re
) approaches zero (
Re \to 0
), viscous forces completely dominate inertial forces. This regime corresponds to very slow flows or highly viscous fluids, where the flow is invariably laminar and smooth. In this limit, the Navier-Stokes equations simplify to the Stokes equations (or creeping flow equations). Conversely, as the Reynolds number approaches infinity (
Re \to ∞
), inertial forces overwhelmingly dominate viscous forces. In this regime, viscosity has a negligible effect on the overall flow behavior, and the flow tends to become highly turbulent, characterized by chaotic eddies and mixing. In this limit, the Navier-Stokes equations can be approximated by the Euler equations, which neglect viscous terms entirely.

reynolds-number-scaling-ident-b08188f8.conceptual_definition:
The Reynolds number (
Re
) is a dimensionless quantity in fluid mechanics used to predict flow patterns in different fluid flow situations. It is the ratio of inertial forces to viscous forces within a fluid that is subjected to relative internal movement due to different fluid velocities.
